WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.395 --> 00:00:04.185
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:06.090
SPEAKER: Network
Intelligence Center

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.039
is Google Cloud's
solution for network

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.830
monitoring across the
cloud and on premise.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.770
It improves user experience,
security, and operations

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.500
by providing a comprehensive
platform for monitoring,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.650
visualizing, and troubleshooting
network health across the cloud

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.330
and data center.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.420
Connectivity Tests is a module
in Network Intelligence Center

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.180
that enables you to do
network verification, which

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.640
is a key part of
intent-based networking.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.710
The idea of verification
is to ensure

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.650
that the intent of the
network designer is being met.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.510
It also allows you
to move from reactive

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.540
to proactive network operations
by predicting what could happen

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.180
when you make a config change,
thus preventing network

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:42.890
outages.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.170
Published sources show
that 75% of network outages

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.560
and performance issues happen
due to misconfiguration.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.240
In a complex Google
Cloud environment

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.390
with a shared VPC, many
firewall rules and routes,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.510
it can be difficult to quickly
identify which configuration

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.480
caused the connectivity issue.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.400
Connectivity Test enables
a user to test connectivity

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.010
between a source and
destination end point.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.630
It also tells you
if the connectivity

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.110
is broken and identifies the
root cause, such as a firewall

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.970
rule or route config.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.970
Connectivity Test performs a
static reachability analysis

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.530
by building a model
of the network

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.050
and then doing packet
simulation on the network based

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.310
on configuration.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.770
Let's see how you can use
on-demand connectivity tests

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.900
with the help of three
different examples.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.110
Say you've run into connectivity
issues between two VM instances

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.068
in different zones in a project.

00:01:30.068 --> 00:01:32.610
You can check the connectivity
between source and destination

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.340
endpoint by creating
a connectivity test.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.650
Click on Create
Connectivity Test.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.090
Pick the protocol ICMP.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:48.410
Select a source VM instance or
an IP address, pick VPC1 US VM,

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.110
and a destination VM
instance slash IP address.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:55.580
Pick VPC1 EU VM,
and click Create.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.970
This saves the test
and also runs it.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:03.170
Once the test is run, it shows
Test Result as Unreachable.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.950
Click on View to check
the result details.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.080
It shows you a detailed
hop-by-hop trace,

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.720
showing how the packet would
traverse through the network.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.330
From the trace, you can see the
packet starts from the source

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.977
VM, goes through an
egress firewall rule,

00:02:16.977 --> 00:02:19.310
and then the packet gets
dropped by the ingress firewall

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.700
rule at the destination VM.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.460
You can click any of the hops
to see more details of the hop.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.510
Here, if you click
Ingress Firewall Rule,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.540
you can see that the
Deny ICMP Firewall

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.820
rule blocked the connectivity.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.580
Right from here, you can go
to the firewall rule page,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.390
update or disable
the firewall rule.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.260
Let's disable the
firewall rule and check

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.370
if this fixes the issue.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.500
Select the test, and run
the connectivity test again

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.170
to verify the impact of
your firewall rule change

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.680
and make sure
connectivity is restored.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.630
After you rerun, the
Test Result shows

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:56.210
Reachable, which tells you
that the connectivity issue is

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.290
fixed.

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.470
Notice how easy it was to
quickly understand the trace,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.510
find out the root
cause, firewall rule,

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.678
and update it, and analyze
whether it fixes the problem?

00:03:05.678 --> 00:03:07.220
Let's look at a more
complex scenario

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.840
where there are
two VPCs connected

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.150
via high-availability VPN.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.130
AJ VPN creates two
tunnels to provide

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.940
four nines of availability.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.740
We'll test connectivity between
a VM in VPC1 connected to a VM

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.350
in VPC2 via HA VPN.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.910
Click on Create
Connectivity Test

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:30.230
and pick the protocol TCP,
source and destination VM,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.210
and click Create.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.270
It shows Test Result
as Unreachable.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:38.012
Click on View to see the trace.

00:03:38.012 --> 00:03:39.470
In this case, you
can see there are

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.810
two traces, which is
expected, since this path is

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.490
through HA VPN.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:48.410
Connectivity Test will show you
multiple traces if they exist.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:51.290
From this trace, you can see
that there is a VPN gateway,

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.930
and the traffic is getting
dropped at the ingress firewall

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.910
rule VPC2, Deny HTTP.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.040
You can go to the
Firewall Rules page

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.490
and fix or disable
the firewall rule.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:07.310
Let's disable it
and rerun the test.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.067
And you can now see that the
connectivity is restored.

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:13.400
You can also check the second,
trace to analyze the path

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.102
and make sure both the
tunnels are set up correctly,

00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:20.310
You can also verify connectivity
between Google Cloud

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.560
and on-prem, or on-prem
to Google Cloud, which

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.608
could be especially useful
during application migration.

00:04:25.608 --> 00:04:27.150
Here we are checking
the connectivity

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.720
between the VM instance on
GCP and an external on-prem IP

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.260
address.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:40.680
Click on Create Connectivity
Test, select Protocol, ICMP,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.020
source VM instance as VPC1 US
VM, and destination IP address.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.810
Once the test
finishes, you can see

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.410
Test Result shows
Reachable, which

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.280
means the connectivity is OK.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.280
You can click on View and
look at the path trace

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.690
to better understand
your network design.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.330
In this case, you
can see the traces

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.000
going through a NAT gateway
and can reach the external IP

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.320
address.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.790
As a user, you can
create and save

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.790
multiple connectivity tests
that reflect your intent,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.290
connectivity that
should be allowed,

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:14.940
and those that
should be blocked.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.070
Here, you can see we
have created and saved

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.810
a few different tests.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.970
Anytime you make a
configuration change,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.490
you can run these tests
to verify the impact

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.500
of your configuration changes.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.420
For some tests,
the intent might be

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.730
to have connectivity
between the endpoints,

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.150
so you'll see those
test results in green.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.980
For other tests,
the intent might

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.440
be to block connectivity
between the endpoints,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.870
so those test results
should be in red.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.720
Anytime you make a
configuration change,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.590
you can run these tests to make
sure your intent stays intact.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.570
Connectivity Test
provides you a quick way

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.670
to do network
verification and ensure

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.000
that any configuration change
does not violate your intent.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.490
It allows you to treat
your configuration as code,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.410
and you can think of
these as unit tests

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.240
that you can run to
prevent network outages

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.480
and also verify your security
slash compliance posture.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:04.520
Congratulations.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.020
You're all set up to start
using the Connectivity Test

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.180
module in network
Intelligence Center

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.820
to do network verification,
quickly diagnose

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.460
connectivity issues due
to misconfiguration,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.150
and predict the impact
of configuration changes.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

